{"tool_name":"scsilicon","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"scsilicon","home":"https://github.com/xikanfeng2/SCSilicon","license":"MIT","summary":"A tool for synthetic single-cell DNA sequencing data generation.","addition_date":"2022-08-17T20:43:36.573661Z","last_update_date":"2024-11-24T15:48:51.998191Z","tool_type":["Library"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:scsilicon","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"fxk@nwpu.edu.cn","name":"Xikang Feng","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0003-4029-3795","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"Lingxi Chen"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Copy number variation detection","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3961"},{"term":"Genotyping","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3196"},{"term":"SNP detection","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0484"}]}],"name":"SCSilicon","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Jennifer","publication":[{"doi":"10.1186/s12864-022-08566-w","metadata":{"abstract":"Background: Single-cell DNA sequencing is getting indispensable in the study of cell-specific cancer genomics. The performance of computational tools that tackle single-cell genome aberrations may be nevertheless undervalued or overvalued, owing to the insufficient size of benchmarking data. In silicon simulation is a cost-effective approach to generate as many single-cell genomes as possible in a controlled manner to make reliable and valid benchmarking. Results: This study proposes a new tool, SCSilicon, which efficiently generates single-cell in silicon DNA reads with minimum manual intervention. SCSilicon automatically creates a set of genomic aberrations, including SNP, SNV, Indel, and CNV. Besides, SCSilicon yields the ground truth of CNV segmentation breakpoints and subclone cell labels. We have manually inspected a series of synthetic variations. We conducted a sanity check of the start-of-the-art single-cell CNV callers and found SCYN was the most robust one. Conclusions: SCSilicon is a user-friendly software package for users to develop and benchmark single-cell CNV callers. Source code of SCSilicon is available at https://github.com/xikanfeng2/SCSilicon.","authors":[{"name":"Chen L."},{"name":"Feng X."}],"citationCount":3,"date":"2022-12-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"BMC Genomics","title":"SCSilicon: a tool for synthetic single-cell DNA sequencing data generation"},"pmcid":"PMC9092674","pmid":"35546390"}],"topic":[{"term":"Copy number variation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3958"},{"term":"DNA polymorphism","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2885"},{"term":"Oncology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_2640"},{"term":"Phylogeny","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0084"},{"term":"Sequencing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3168"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"SCSilicon","home":"https://bio.tools/scsilicon","license":"MIT","summary":"A tool for synthetic single-cell DNA sequencing data generation.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:scsilicon/lib/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":null,"interpreted":true,"license":null,"public":true,"readme":false,"repository":{"anonymous":null,"online":null}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":5,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2023"},{"count":1,"year":"2024"},{"count":1,"year":"2026"},{"count":2,"year":"2025"}],"doi":"10.1186/s12864-022-08566-w","pmcid":"PMC9092674","pmid":"35546390","ref_count":29,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"2009"},{"count":1,"year":"2011"},{"count":1,"year":"2014"},{"count":1,"year":"2015"},{"count":1,"year":"2